# React handlers

JSX event props use a named `handle…` function when the body is more than a trivial expression. The name matches the prop: `onClick={handleClick}`, `onOpen={handleOpen}`.

Inline a **single expression** — pass a hook result through, or one call:

```tsx
<Checkbox onChange={toggleAll} />
<Button onClick={() => zip([...selected])} />
```

Name it when there are **guards, branches, or more than one statement**:

```tsx
const handleRowOpen = ({ id }: { id: string }) => {
  if (!canOpen) {
    return;
  }

  onOpen({ id });
};

<Table.Row onOpen={handleRowOpen} />
```

The handler lives in the compose edge or leaf that renders the prop. Shared work stays in `util/` or a hook; the handler only calls it.

Hook-returned functions follow the same shape: name `handleX` in the body, compose it in `return { … }` (`react-feature-hooks.mdc`). Do not wrap with `useCallback`.

List rows use a curried `handleX` (`react-feature-hooks.mdc`).
